---
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import DraftReadout from "../components/DraftReadout.astro";
---
<Page
  title="Soundings"
  dek="A sounding is a measurement. Salvage reports what each change does in numbers, on named machines, with the method attached."
>
  <DraftReadout />
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Why draft</h2>
    <p>
      On a ship, the draft is how deep the hull sits in the water, read off numbers painted on the bow. A ship that
      carries less rides higher. Memory works the same way, so that's how we report it: draft before, draft after, in
      gigabytes.
    </p>

    <h2>What every sounding records</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The machine.</strong> Processor, graphics card, memory, storage and operating system.</li>
      <li><strong>The software.</strong> Engine version, the project, and the build configuration.</li>
      <li><strong>The scenario.</strong> What was run, for how long, and how many times.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>The numbers.</strong> Before and after, with the spread across runs, not only the best one.
      </li>
      <li><strong>The method.</strong> Enough detail that you can repeat it on your own machine.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A number without those is a rumor. If a change only helps on one kind of machine, the sounding says so.
    </p>

    <h2>Take your own</h2>
    <p>
      Real machines beat our guesses. <a href="/charts/salvage-soundings/"><code>salvage soundings</code></a> reads
      the memreports and LLM CSVs Unreal writes, and <a href="/charts/capturing-memory-runs/">Capture memory runs you
      can compare</a> shows how to take them. Results from your hardware are welcome as issues
      on <a href="https://github.com/Chapterists/salvage/issues">GitHub</a>, with the same details as above.
    </p>
  </div>
</Page>
